---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Trading Psychology — The Biases That Cost Money";
const description =
  "Loss aversion, the disposition effect, recency and hindsight bias: the specific " +
  "mental errors that show up in trading records, and what actually helps.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Trading / Trading psychology">
  <h1>Trading psychology</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Most of what gets written under this heading is motivational. What follows is
    narrower: the specific, documented biases that show up as money leaving an
    account, and the small number of things that measurably help.
  </p>

  <h2>The disposition effect</h2>
  <p>
    The tendency to sell winners early and hold losers too long. It is among the
    best-documented findings in behavioural finance and shows up in retail
    brokerage records across every market studied.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The mechanism is simple: selling a winner realises a gain, which feels good.
    Selling a loser realises a loss, which converts a "temporary dip" into an
    admitted mistake. So the loser is kept, often until it is much larger.
  </p>
  <p>
    The effect is exactly backwards for a trend-following approach, which depends
    on a few large winners to pay for many small losses. Cut the winners short and
    the arithmetic stops working — see
    <a href="/learn/risk-management/">expectancy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Loss aversion</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Losses are experienced roughly twice as intensely as equivalent gains. The
    practical consequence is that a position at a loss generates enough discomfort
    to distort judgement about it — which is precisely when clear judgement is
    needed.
  </p>

  <h2>Recency</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>after_a_winning_streak</code><span class="meta">the dangerous one</span></div>
      <p>Confidence rises, position sizes creep up, rules get relaxed. The largest
        losses in most trading records arrive immediately after the best runs, for
        exactly this reason.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>after_a_losing_streak</code><span class="meta">equally costly</span></div>
      <p>Signals get skipped. Since a small number of large winners carry the
        result, skipping a few can turn a profitable year into a flat one — and
        the ones skipped are not random, they are the ones taken after losses,
        which is when opportunities often cluster.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Hindsight bias</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    On a completed chart every turning point looks obvious, which makes the past
    seem far more predictable than it was. This is also what makes backtests
    seductive and what makes "I knew that was going to happen" a near-universal
    and near-universally false feeling.
  </p>
  <p>
    The only defence is writing decisions down <em>before</em> outcomes are known.
    A record made in advance is immune to reinterpretation; memory is not.
  </p>

  <h2>Confirmation bias</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Once a position exists, information supporting it feels more convincing than
    information against it. You will notice the analyst who agrees and dismiss the
    one who does not. The discipline that helps is writing down, at entry,
    specifically what would make you wrong — and then treating it as binding.
  </p>

  <h2>What actually helps</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>decide_before_you_are_exposed</code><span class="meta">the core idea</span></div>
      <p>Entry, size, stop and target written down before the position exists.
        Every rule here works by moving the decision to a moment when you are not
        under pressure.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>keep_a_record</code><span class="meta">most effective single habit</span></div>
      <p>Every trade with its reason and outcome. It converts impressions into
        evidence, and the evidence is frequently unflattering in useful ways —
        most people discover their instincts about which trades work are wrong.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>size_so_it_is_boring</code><span class="meta">underrated</span></div>
      <p>If a position is large enough to disturb you, it is large enough to make
        you act badly. Most psychology problems are position sizing problems
        wearing a different hat.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>expect_the_losing_streak</code><span class="meta">calibration</span></div>
      <p>At a 40% win rate, eight consecutive losses is a normal event over a few
        hundred trades, not evidence the approach has stopped working. Knowing the
        number in advance makes it survivable.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The thing that does not help</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Trying to feel differently. You will not stop finding losses painful, and
    advice to "control your emotions" is not actionable. What works is
    <strong>structural</strong> — rules written in advance, sizes small enough
    that the emotion stays manageable, and a record that tells you what actually
    happened rather than what you remember happening.
  </p>

